// PARAM: --analysis interval
// Both branches of the if end in a call to the same procedure, so both calls
// resume at the same CFG node: the compiler emits pp4 --call-to-return f--> pp6
// and pp5 --call-to-return f--> pp6. That node's equation therefore carries two
// call actions to f, both routing to the same context, so one right-hand-side
// evaluation performs two side effects to the same callee-entry seed key.
//
// Two side effects to one key from one right-hand-side evaluation used to
// livelock: the per-origin update was applied eagerly per side effect against
// the running accumulate, so the dead branch's write landed first carrying
// bottom, dropped the seed below what the live branch had established, and the
// two writes destabilized the callee alternately in a fixed 10-step cycle. The
// contract that settles it -- one right-hand-side evaluation contributes one
// aggregated value per target key -- is stated in the sibling case 10.
//
// Reversing the branch polarity hides the ordering entirely, so this case pins
// the polarity that exercised it, under --context none, where the default
// Interval configuration reaches this shape.
void f(n) {
  return n
}
void main() {
  int32 a, x;
  x := 5;
  if (x < 2) {
    a := f(1)
  } else {
    a := f(1)
  };
  __voblint_check(x == 5) // PROVED
}
